! ============================================================================
!  INFORM 6 SKELETON  —  Frotzsmith starter (Standard Library)
! ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
!  A bare, compilable starting point. Replace the title and author, then build
!  your world by adding rooms and objects between VerbLib and Grammar.
!
!  Everything that starts with `!` (like this line) is a COMMENT: the compiler
!  skips from the `!` to the end of the line, so comments cost nothing in the
!  finished game. (A `!` inside a "quoted string" is just a character.) This
!  file is written to be read top to bottom: every line of real code below has
!  a comment above it saying what it is, why it is there, and what would
!  happen without it.
! ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
!  KEY CONCEPTS IN THIS FILE
!  ─────────────────────────
!  • Constant          — sets a named value at compile time
!  • Include           — pastes another source file in-place (the library)
!  • [ Initialise; ]   — a routine the library calls once at game start
!  • Object            — the fundamental Inform 6 building block (rooms & things)
!  • has light         — the attribute that lets the player see inside a room
!
!  THE SHAPE OF EVERY INFORM 6 GAME
!  ────────────────────────────────
!  An Inform 6 source file is a list of DIRECTIVES — top-level statements that
!  each end with a semicolon `;`. The ones used here are:
!    Constant  NAME value;           a named value fixed when you compile
!    Include   "file";               read another source file at this point
!    [ Name ... ];                   a ROUTINE (a function) called Name
!    Object    id "name" with ...;   an object: a room, a thing, a person
!  The compiler reads the file once, from top to bottom, and turns it into a
!  story file (.z5 here) that a Z-machine interpreter plays.
!
!  STANDARD LIBRARY vs PUNYINFORM
!  ────────────────────────────────
!  The Standard Library (this file) is included via three explicit Includes:
!    Include "Parser";    ← parses typed commands into actions
!    Include "VerbLib";   ← defines the built-in verbs (take, drop, examine…)
!    Include "Grammar";   ← comes LAST; wires verb words to the actions
!  The library enforces that order itself: each file checks which ones came
!  before it and prints an error message if they are out of order.
!  PunyInform replaces all three with two files (globals.h / puny.h) and uses
!  the constant INITIAL_LOCATION_VALUE instead of setting `location` by hand
!  inside Initialise. See puny-skeleton.inf for the PunyInform equivalent.
!
!  HOW THE GAME FLOWS
!    One room, the Starting Room, and nothing in it yet. LOOK shows it again;
!    everything else gets the library's stock replies. Copy this file to begin
!    a game of your own.
!
!  WHAT THE PLAYER SEES AT START, IN ORDER
!    1. "Your story begins here..."   (printed by Initialise, below)
!    2. The banner: Story, Headline, release/serial/library line
!    3. The first room description (the library does an automatic LOOK)
!    4. "Obvious exits: ..."          (added by Frotzsmith's Exits.h; this
!                                       room has no exits, so it prints nothing)
! ============================================================================

! --- Game identity constants -------------------------------------------------
!  `Constant` declares a compile-time name/value pair. These two are special:
!  the Standard Library's Banner routine prints `Story` (the title, in bold)
!  and then `Headline` (the subtitle) when the game starts; `Story` also
!  appears in messages such as "MY GAME is now in its verbose mode". The `^`
!  character inside strings is a newline. (The status bar at the top shows
!  the current room's name and the score/moves, not the title.)
!
!  By convention they come first. If either is still undefined when Grammar
!  is included, the library gives it the value 0 and the banner simply
!  leaves that line blank.
!  A constant can hold a number (`Constant MAX_SCORE 10;`) or, like these,
!  a string. Constants never change while the game runs; for values that do
!  change, Inform uses `Global` variables (see the Haunted House sample).

Constant Story "MY GAME";
Constant Headline "^An interactive fiction by Anonymous.^";

! --- Standard Library includes ----------------------------------------------
!  Include "Parser" must come before VerbLib. It pulls in the parser engine
!  that turns raw input ("take lamp") into an action (Take, noun=lamp). It
!  also defines the attributes (`light`, `static`, ...) and properties
!  (`description`, `n_to`, ...) that the objects below use, which is why the
!  objects have to come after it.

Include "Parser";

!  Include "VerbLib" adds the standard verb handlers: Take, Drop, Examine,
!  Inventory, Go, Look, Put, etc. Place any new Object/Class definitions AFTER
!  this include and BEFORE "Grammar".

Include "VerbLib";

! --- Initialise routine -----------------------------------------------------
!  A ROUTINE is written `[ Name local1 local2; statement; statement; ];`.
!  The square brackets enclose it, the first `;` ends the header (the name
!  plus any local variables), and each statement ends with its own `;`.
!
!  The library calls [ Initialise; ] exactly once, just before the first prompt.
!  It is an ENTRY POINT: a routine with a fixed name that the library looks
!  for and calls at a fixed moment. You never call it yourself.
!  In the Standard Library you MUST set the global `location` here to tell the
!  parser which room the player starts in. (A global is a variable visible
!  everywhere; `location` is one the library declares and keeps up to date.)
!  Leave it unset and the library has nowhere to put the player.
!
!  A string literal used as a statement (the line starting with `"^Your..."`)
!  acts as a `print` + newline + `rtrue` in Inform 6 shorthand — it prints the
!  welcome message and returns true, ending the routine.
!  The return value matters here: if Initialise returns 2 the library skips
!  the banner; any other value (true = 1 here) lets the banner print after
!  your message.

[ Initialise;
    location = StartRoom;
    "^Your story begins here...^";
];

! --- Rooms and objects -------------------------------------------------------
!  Every room is an Object. The syntax is:
!    Object  identifier  "short name"
!      with  property value,          ! WITH: the object's PROPERTIES
!            property value,          !   (separated by commas)
!      has   attribute attribute;     ! HAS: the object's ATTRIBUTES
!  and the whole declaration ends at its semicolon.
!
!  The IDENTIFIER (StartRoom) is the name your code uses for the object, as
!  in `location = StartRoom;` above. The first string after it is the SHORT
!  NAME (shown in the status bar and as the heading on >LOOK).
!
!  PROPERTIES vs ATTRIBUTES — the two kinds of data an object carries:
!    property  — a named slot holding a VALUE: a number, a string, another
!                object, or a routine. `description "..."` is a property.
!    attribute — a yes/no FLAG that is either set or not. `light` is one.
!                Code tests it with `if (obj has light)` and changes it
!                while the game runs with `give obj light;`/`give obj ~light;`.
!
!  `with description "…"` supplies the long description printed by >LOOK or
!  when the player enters for the first time.
!
!  `has light` is an *attribute*. Without it the room is dark and the player
!  cannot see anything inside it. Most outdoor locations and lit indoor rooms
!  need `has light`. Give a carried light source (lamp) the `light` attribute
!  instead to make the light portable. (Try deleting `has light` below: the
!  game opens on "Darkness / It is pitch dark".)

Object StartRoom "Starting Room"
  with description "An empty room, waiting for your imagination.",
  has  light;

! ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
!  Add your rooms and objects here.
!
!  ROOM-TO-ROOM CONNECTIONS
!  Use directional properties on the room object, e.g.:
!    n_to AnotherRoom,   ← typing NORTH moves the player to AnotherRoom
!    s_to YetAnother,    ← SOUTH goes to YetAnother
!  Available directions: n_to s_to e_to w_to ne_to nw_to se_to sw_to
!                        u_to (UP) d_to (DOWN) in_to out_to
!  A connection is one-way: give the other room the matching property (e.g.
!  s_to back to this room) if the player should be able to return.
!
!  CHILD OBJECTS (things inside rooms)
!  Use `Object -> ` (arrow notation) to place an object inside the preceding
!  room or container at compile time:
!    Object -> pebble "small pebble"
!      with name 'small' 'pebble',
!           description "Just a grey pebble.";
!  `name` lists the words (in 'single quotes') the player can type to refer
!  to the object; without it the parser cannot recognise the object at all.
!
!  ATTRIBUTES QUICK REFERENCE
!  has light      — room/object is lit (player can see)
!  has static     — object can't be taken (prints "That's fixed in place.")
!  has scenery    — can't be taken AND is left out of the room's object list
!                   (mention it in the room description instead)
!  has container  — object can hold other objects; add `open` to start open
!  has supporter  — object things can be placed ON (table, shelf)
!  has openable   — player can OPEN/CLOSE it (use with container or door)
!  has open       — object starts in the open state
!  has concealed  — object is present but not listed in room descriptions
! ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

! --- Grammar (must be last) -------------------------------------------------
!  Include "Grammar" is the final library include. It defines the grammar
!  tables that map verb words ('take', 'get', 'look' ...) to actions (Take,
!  Look ...). It must come after VerbLib (the library checks this).
!
!  Your own grammar: a brand-new verb (`Verb 'xyzzy' ...`) may go before or
!  after this include, but `Extend` (adding a pattern to a verb the library
!  already has, such as 'push') must go AFTER it, because the verb it extends
!  does not exist until Grammar has defined it. See the Custom Grammar sample.
!
!  Include "Exits" is Frotzsmith's own small helper (not part of the official
!  library). It defines the LookRoutine entry point, which the library calls
!  after every room description, and prints "Obvious exits: north, south."
!  Remove the line and the game still works; rooms just stop listing exits.

Include "Exits";    ! Frotzsmith: auto-list obvious exits (see Exits.h)
Include "Grammar";
